window.docContents[194].resolve({"/Colorings/#section-colourings-cliques-and-independent-sets":{"contents":"Any k-clique in a graph requires at least k colours to colour it, so the clique\nnumber of a graph is at most its chromatic number.\n\n#check cliqueNum_le_chromaticNumber\nexample {s : Finset V} (h : G.IsClique s) : #s ≤ χ G := h.card_le_chromaticNumber\n\n\nSo if χ G < n then G is n-clique-free.\n\n#check cliqueFree_of_chromaticNumber_lt\n\n\nIf a clique has at least as many vertices as there are colours, then every colour\nappears on the clique.\n\n#check Coloring.surjOn_of_card_le_isClique\n\n\nA proper colouring is injective on any clique: two distinct vertices of a clique\nare adjacent, so they receive different colours. Since a colouring is a homomorphism,\nthis is a special case of Hom.injOn_of_isClique from\nFinite Cliques, which dot notation finds for us.\n\nexample {α : Type*} (c : G.Coloring α) {s : Set V} (hs : G.IsClique s) :\n    Set.InjOn c s :=\n  c.injOn_of_isClique hs\n\n\nIf H is not n-colourable and G is n-colourable then G cannot contain a copy\nof H, e.g. a bipartite graph cannot contain a triangle.\n\n#check free_of_colorable\n\n\nWhat can we say about maximally clique-free graphs? Suppose that G is\n(r + 1)-clique-free but adding any edge creates an (r + 1)-clique. Then G is\nr-colourable iff it is complete multipartite.\n\n#check colorable_iff_isCompleteMultipartite_of_maximal_cliqueFree\n\n\nA graph is complete multipartite if its vertices split into parts so that two\nvertices are adjacent iff they lie in different parts. Mathlib defines this as\ntransitivity of non-adjacency in G. Non-adjacency is always reflexive (there are\nno loops) and symmetric, so transitivity makes it an equivalence relation, and its\nequivalence classes are the parts.\n\n#source IsCompleteMultipartite\n\n\nColour classes give a bound in the other direction, in terms of independent sets.\nIf G is n-colourable then its vertices are covered by n colour classes, each\nan independent set of at most ⍺ G vertices, so G has at most n * ⍺ G vertices.\nNo finiteness assumption is needed: if V is infinite then Nat.card V is the junk\nvalue 0, so the inequality holds trivially, and we dispose of this case first.\n\ntheorem Colorable.natCard_le_mul_indepNum {n : ℕ} (h : G.Colorable n) :\n    Nat.card V ≤ n * ⍺ G := by\n  classical\n  cases finite_or_infinite V with\n  | inr => simp -- Nat.card V is the junk value 0\n  | inl =>\n  have := Fintype.ofFinite V\n  obtain ⟨C⟩ := h\n  calc Nat.card V = ∑ i, #{v | C v = i} := by\n        rw [Nat.card_eq_fintype_card, ← card_univ,\n          card_eq_sum_card_fiberwise fun v _ => mem_univ (C v)]\n    _ ≤ ∑ _i : Fin n, ⍺ G := sum_le_sum fun i _ => IsIndepSet.card_le_indepNum <| by\n        simpa [Coloring.colorClass] using C.isIndepSet_colorClass i\n    _ = n * ⍺ G := by simp\n\n\nTaking n = χ G shows that a finite graph has at most χ G * ⍺ G vertices. As\nbefore, the Lean statement needs no finiteness assumption, since it holds trivially\nwhen V is infinite. Since χ G takes values in ℕ∞, the statement lives in ℕ∞:\nthe natural numbers Nat.card V and ⍺ G are silently cast to ℕ∞ (compare\nCounting In Enat Card).\n\nFor finite V we cannot apply Colorable.natCard_le_mul_indepNum with n = χ G\ndirectly, since n must be a natural number. This is a job for the lift tactic,\nwhich moves a value from ℕ∞ down to ℕ once we know that it is finite. Given a\nproof that χ G ≠ ⊤ (chromaticNumber_ne_top_of_finite),\nlift χ G to ℕ using … with n hn introduces a natural number n with\nhn : ↑n = χ G and replaces χ G by ↑n in the goal. (We must name n here,\nsince χ G is an expression rather than a variable; when lifting a variable x,\nlift reuses the name x.) Now G is n-colourable by\nchromaticNumber_le_iff_colorable, and exact_mod_cast transfers the inequality\nfrom ℕ to ℕ∞.\n\ntheorem natCard_le_chromaticNumber_mul_indepNum : Nat.card V ≤ χ G * ⍺ G := by\n  cases finite_or_infinite V with\n  | inr => simp -- Nat.card V is the junk value 0\n  | inl =>\n  lift χ G to ℕ using G.chromaticNumber_ne_top_of_finite with n hn\n  exact_mod_cast (chromaticNumber_le_iff_colorable.mp hn.ge).natCard_le_mul_indepNum\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Colorings","header":"7.3. Colourings, Cliques and Independent Sets","id":"/Colorings/#section-colourings-cliques-and-independent-sets"},"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#chapter-decidability-finiteness-and-degrees":{"contents":"namespace SimpleGraph\n\n\nThis chapter is about computing with graphs in Lean, and about the typeclass\nmachinery that makes computation possible. We begin with decidability: the contrast\nbetween Bool and Prop, the Decidable class, the commands and tactics that use\nit (#eval, decide and native_decide, and what each of them trusts), and the\nDecidableRel G.Adj instances required to decide adjacency. We then turn to\nfiniteness, starting from the hierarchy List → Multiset → Finset and arriving\nat Fintype, which is data, and Finite, which is a proposition; along the way we\ncompare the ways Mathlib has of counting the elements of a finite type or set. A\npoint we return to more than once is that because Fintype instances are data, the\nsame type can receive two different instances by two different routes of typeclass\ninference. This is known as a diamond, after the shape of the two routes meeting at\none type. Such a diamond is a nuisance rather than a feature: the two instances are\nalways equal, but not by definition, so a theorem stated using one instance will\nnot apply to a goal that uses the other. We will see how to state theorems so that\nthis does not happen.\n\nThe second half applies all this to graphs. We study finite neighbourhoods and vertex\ndegrees (including minimum and maximum degree, the handshake lemma and regular\ngraphs). We then compute with two families of graphs, hypercubes and Mycielskians,\nbuilding the DecidableRel and Fintype instances needed both to evaluate and to\nprove facts about their degrees and edge counts.\n\nFinally, an advanced section on degree sequences defines the degree multiset\nof a graph, asks which multisets are graphic, states, without proof, the\nErdős–Gallai and Havel–Hakimi characterizations, and turns the latter into a function\nthat computes the edges of a graph with a given graphic degree sequence.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean","header":"4. Decidability, Finiteness and Degrees","id":"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#chapter-decidability-finiteness-and-degrees"}});